{
  "title": "Direct Preference Optimization",
  "authors": [
    "Rafael Rafailov",
    "Archit Sharma",
    "Eric Mitchell",
    "Stefano Ermon",
    "Christopher D. Manning",
    "Chelsea Finn"
  ],
  "year": 2024,
  "arxivId": "2305.18290",
  "abstract": "Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) is a new approach to fine-tuning language models that avoids the need for reinforcement learning. By leveraging a particular parameterization of the reward model, DPO enables the extraction of the optimal policy in closed form, allowing for optimization with a simple classification loss. This approach eliminates the need for sampling from the language model during fine-tuning and reduces computational costs.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "Imagine you have a language model that can generate text, but you want it to generate text that aligns with human preferences. Existing methods use reinforcement learning to achieve this, but it can be complex and unstable. DPO offers a simpler approach by directly optimizing the language model to satisfy human preferences without the need for reinforcement learning. <strong>Key insight</strong>: DPO uses a change of variables to define the preference loss as a function of the policy directly, allowing for optimization with a simple binary cross-entropy objective.",
      "visualization": {
